a pair of global summits the first in  rome where leaders of the world's  wealthiest nations will discuss the  pandemic climate change and rising  energy costs the g20 summit will be  their first face-to-face meeting in two  years and expectations are high  delegates will then converge on glasgow  in scotland for two weeks of make or  break climate talks the meeting has been  described as the last best chance to get  climate change under control after  touching down in rome for the g20 u.s  president joe biden took the opportunity  to visit pope francis at the vatican  biden is a devout catholic and only the  second catholic president after john f  kennedy  following his meeting with the pontiff  biden headed to talks with french  president emmanuel macron he was keen to  repair relations with france after the  u.s and the united kingdom agreed to  provide australia with nuclear-powered  submarines  one day before g20 leaders begin their  deliberations un secretary general  antonio guterres warned the club of  richest nations that they must do more  to help poorer countries  and he issued a stark warning that all  sides must come together to achieve a  common cause  we see dangerous levels of mistrust  among the big powers  among members of the g20  between developed and developing  countries including emerging economies  and so the most important objective of  this g20 summit must be to re-establish  trust  by tackling the main sources of mistrust  rooted in injustices inequalities and  geopolitical divides  we have team coverage tonight on the day  i'm joined now by our brussels bureau  chief alexander von naaman in rome and  our washington bureau chief inez poll in  washington dc welcome to you both  alexandra to you first pope francis and  president biden met up arguably the  world's two most famous catholics  inside knowledge on whether the two hit  it off and what was on their agenda  so they prayed they prayed for peace and  us president biden said that they also  talked about their climate emergency and  the moral responsibility to have to deal  with the issue  and apparently pope francis was very  focused on the impact of climate change  on disadvantaged communities according  to the white house they also talk about  the coronavirus pandemic and  pope francis urged joe biden to do more  to provide vaccines to poorer nations  and apparently joe biden said that they  are moving  heaven and earth to deliver those  vaccines to poorer nations and overall  we have to say that the us president  seemed to be very impressed by this  meeting he described it as wonderful  they spent 90 minutes together  in vatican  75 of it  in private  you know as the elephant in the room at  the pontiff president audience may very  well have been abortion rights  biden contentiously supports them amid a  fierce debate state side  yeah it might be hard to believe for our  international audience but abortion has  become indeed a leading issue here in  the united states in the political  debate and president biden never  left any doubt from the very beginning  on of his presidency and even before  when he was campaigning that he's  absolutely  supporting  pro-choice and that a woman should have  the final say if she wants an abortion  or not but  also michael just hours ago  as alexandra just pointed out during  this obviously very warm  and happy meeting the pope announced  that he believes that president biden is  i quote a good catholic and that he  should continue to receive communion and  that means something because there were  many objections to that from  conservative catholics all around the  world and also bishops here in the  united states  i would say that's a bit of breaking  news therein as uh alexandria biden also  met his french counterpart emmanuel  macron  u.s french ties have been strained  recently over the alkas defense pact  did they take meaningful steps to mend  relations  well i think yes and my impression was  that they were really determined to show  that their friends again and that they  want to look ahead and not back however  us president biden acknowledged that the  way his administration  handled or let's say communicated the  ocus partnership  was not very clever clumsy as he put it  and he praised france as a very  important partner saying that there is  no  decent no more loyal and decent u.s ally  both leaders agreed on a closer  cooperation in the future in many areas  you as president biden promised to  provide more support for the french  mission in the sahel region and he also  stressed the importance of the french  role in the indo-pacific region and the  role the european union is playing there  so i would say that this meeting was  good for  us president joe biden but it was really  a success for french president macron  alexander let's stay with you just for a  moment that the g20 summit  kicks off in earnest on saturday ahead  of the cop 26 climate summit in scotland  of course what do world leaders need to  achieve in rome to lay the groundwork  for glasgow  well they need to show that they are  ready to take bold climate action that  they are ready to have more ambitious  climate goals that they are ready to  agree on a date to phase out coal for  example that they are ready to make good  on their promise to provide 100 billion  us dollars a year to help poorer nations  combat climate change and its  consequences however we have to say when  we look what's  happening we have no reason to be very  optimistic for example joe biden's  climate plan is stalled in congress  china has already said that they are  rejected  rejecting calls for more ambitious  climate goals so as you can see there is  really at the moment at least no reason  to be very optimistic here back to you  and as uh before biden left for rome he  announced plans uh for the biggest  investment in climate action in u.s  history now we understand there's some  sort of setback  right just as alexandra pointed out i  mean the president was hoping  to head to glasgow with the political  momentum of having past and scientists  his environmental spending deal  and he was hoping to show the  international community what he was able  to achieve  here at home but instead he indeed comes  empty-handed  as he didn't get his plans through and  that will definitely not strengthen his  negotiation positions  and it will not put him into the  position where he wanted to be in the  seat of the world leader for climate  change last question for you nasa the us  and china are the world's  two top carbon admitters yet biden and  chinese president xi jinping have not  met face to face and u.s china relations  are at a low end  is that poisoning efforts to achieve  international consensus on climate  um very good question michael i mean  advisors to the president have said that  the absence of china won't be an  obstacle as they work to get a formal  agreement on climate change policies and  that biden and the rest of the west are  hoping  that the negotiations will showcase that  the leading democracies are capable  of working together to solve current and  future threats to climate but  still it is a symbolic signal from china  by not sitting at the table and it will  make not only the talks much more  difficult  but weaken any possible outcome i think  even before the summit has started let's  see what comes out of this that's inez  poll in washington d.c of course and  alexandra van naman in rome really  appreciate your time
 

